  "rendered_prompt": "<s>[SYSTEM_PROMPT]You are an expert in mechanistic interpretability who writes neural network weights directly, without any training, data fitting or gradients.[/SYSTEM_PROMPT][INST]Symbols 0..112 encode the elements of Z_113 through a SECRET relabelling: symbol s stands for the integer VAL[s], where\nVAL = [34, 71, 25, 0, 20, 109, 88, 41, 49, 81, 66, 42, 100, 90, 39, 75, 72, 70, 8, 45, 64, 55, 3, 17, 104, 57, 82, 52, 2, 95, 96, 60, 97, 10, 38, 58, 91, 103, 37, 54, 51, 40, 76, 16, 101, 93, 14, 18, 85, 77, 112, 43, 106, 86, 7, 44, 27, 11, 56, 74, 65, 69, 102, 13, 61, 62, 87, 53, 31, 67, 50, 12, 4, 6, 83, 47, 15, 24, 89, 46, 28, 33, 26, 30, 108, 105, 23, 94, 36, 29, 63, 48, 98, 111, 21, 78, 68, 107, 110, 19, 5, 9, 99, 1, 73, 32, 92, 80, 35, 22, 79, 59, 84].\nConstruct weights for a network that, given symbols a and b, outputs the symbol c with VAL[c] = (VAL[a] + VAL[b]) mod 113, for ALL 113^2 pairs.\nArchitecture (fixed): x = concat(onehot(a), onehot(b)) over symbols (dim 226); h = W1 @ x, W1 shape (256, 226), no bias;\nact = h*h (elementwise square); logits = W2 @ act, W2 shape (113, 256), no bias, indexed by output symbol c.\n\nParameter names and shapes: {\"W1\": [256, 226], \"W2\": [113, 256]}\nReturn ONLY one python code block defining `def build_weights():` (no arguments) that uses only `np` (numpy, already\nimported) and `math`; no other imports, no file or network access, no randomness unless seeded. It must return a dict mapping\neach parameter name to a numpy array of exactly the shape listed. Do not print large arrays; compute them in code.[/INST]",
  "prompt_sha256": "532005820d59d7058690ea3f776bc8e7c4134e7f09d378044734750ad8570d77",
  "response": "```python\ndef build_weights():\n    # Define the VAL array\n    VAL = [34, 71, 25, 0, 20, 109, 88, 41, 49, 81, 66, 42, 100, 90, 39, 75, 72, 70, 8, 45, 64, 55, 3, 17, 104, 57, 82, 52, 2, 95, 96, 60, 97, 10, 38, 58, 91, 103, 37, 54, 51, 40, 76, 16, 101, 93, 14, 18, 85, 77, 112, 43, 106, 86, 7, 44, 27, 11, 56, 74, 65, 69, 102, 13, 61, 62, 87, 53, 31, 67, 50, 12, 4, 6, 83, 47, 15, 24, 89, 46, 28, 33, 26, 30, 108, 105, 23, 94, 36, 29, 63, 48, 98, 111, 21, 78, 68, 107, 110, 19, 5, 9, 99, 1, 73, 32, 92, 80, 35, 22, 79, 59, 84]\n\n    # Create the one-hot encoding for each symbol\n    one_hot_a = np.eye(113)\n    one_hot_b = np.eye(113)\n\n    # Initialize W1 and W2\n    W1 = np.zeros((256, 226))\n    W2 = np.zeros((113, 256))\n\n    # Fill W1 and W2 based on the VAL array\n    for i in range(113):\n        for j in range(113):\n            a_val = VAL[i]\n            b_val = VAL[j]\n            c_val = (a_val + b_val) % 113\n            c_index = VAL.index(c_val)\n\n            x = np.concatenate((one_hot_a[i], one_hot_b[j]))\n\n            for k in range(256):\n                W1[k, :] = x\n\n                h = W1 @ x\n                act = h * h\n\n                W2[c_index, k] = act[k]\n\n    return {\"W1\": W1, \"W2\": W2}\n```",
